\title{The algebraic branch: interpolation to rank sums}
\taxon{Overview}
\meta{dependencies}{None.}
\meta{provenance}{Expository organization and source review, 9 October 2026.}

\p{As a warm-up, [a coordinate-by-coordinate count](cn-0073) proves the target inequality when all subspaces share a coordinate basis. The [comparison with Scott and Wilmer](cn-0072) explains the additional difficulty for arbitrary subspaces.}

\p{Let #{W} be a finite-dimensional real vector space. We use [univariate polynomial maps](cn-0064) #{P:\mathbb R\to W}: after choosing coordinates in #{W}, each coordinate of #{P(t)} is a scalar polynomial in #{t}. Choose distinct real [interpolation nodes](cn-0063) #{t_i} and require the value #{P(t_i)} to lie in a prescribed subspace #{U_i\subseteq W}. The route is evaluation dimensions, [stagewise adapted columns and minors](cn-0078), rank loss at the nodes, multiplicity of polynomial determinants, and a degree count. The [polynomial-matrix definition](cn-0077) explains the matrices and their evaluated ranks. Graphs of linear maps then turn intersection dimensions into matrix ranks.}

\p{These maps are auxiliary interpolation devices for the subspaces. They do not approximate the drawing's edge curves. The space #{W} used in the rank-sum application is #{\mathbb R^d\oplus\mathbb R^d}; the drawing plane is a separate space. We follow the paper's real notation here, but [the algebraic inequality holds over any field](cn-0065).}

